# Staged Sketch collector hardening

This directory proposes a collector change only. Production implementation, tests, documentation and results have not been edited. The existing `728f28d2aa5d7ebf1155db9a553f60d67029df46ddb512c15135ddf431333a91` geometry receipt, its original observations and raw28-test output are retained exactly in `baseline/`. The existing collector remains bound to that historical receipt; the candidate does not replace or reinterpret it.

`candidate/verify-sketch-geometry.mjs` is the exact old collector with bounded-child orchestration and helper fingerprints added. It is intended to replace the production script only after review, alongside `candidate/sketch-collector-process.mjs`. Its relative production imports are preserved, so it is not runnable from the staging location. Syntax validation and synthetic helper execution are separate from a future source-bound geometry run. No geometry28 run was repeated for this proposal.

The helper starts only the current Node executable, with captured argument/environment arrays, no shell and no inherited `NODE_TEST_CONTEXT`. Default bounds are30 seconds, retained stdout4MiB and stderr1MiB, a5-second cleanup-helper deadline and a11-second close-event guard. Configurable hard maxima are120 seconds,8MiB per output stream and10-second cleanup. A cap or deadline failure keeps clipped raw bytes plus source-bound diagnostics in the collector-owned fresh observations directory. Existing evidence files are exclusive writes. Publication requires a closed successful child and unchanged sources; the existing study inventory, app/helper/docs and unchanged-baseline checks remain in the collector. Sources are hashed using bounded streams (8MiB per file,64MiB aggregate,128 identities).

On Windows, cleanup targets only the PID from the owned child object with the native system `taskkill /T /F`. An actual owned grandchild was terminated in the synthetic regression. A failed cleanup attempt can fall back to signaling the owned root; diagnostics expressly withhold descendant guarantees in that case or when the parent has already exited. This does not provide atomic Windows Job Object isolation, cannot eliminate PID reuse races or catch detached descendants after the parent exits, and does not bound OS buffers or total process memory. The POSIX detached-process-group branch is implemented but was not executed by this Windows validation. The timeout covers the child execution, not archive bootstrap, filesystem operations or the additional trusted in-process legacy oracle; those prior collector operations are unchanged.

The geometry receipt's35 directly counted helper calls cover its17 geometry contexts (diamond8, size8, restore18, serialization1). The legacy six-element restoration corpus is separate, includes further predicates/serializer/restoration calls, and runs once inside the child suite and once again in the collector after it. The candidate's future receipt explicitly labels this accounting; it does not aggregate the legacy calls into the historical35.

The synthetic suite exercises exact caps, stdout and stderr growth, a stalled runner, an owned process tree, ordinary failure, spawn failure, caller-input capture and inherited test-context cleanup, source mutation/deletion, preexisting-evidence protection and source-byte overflow. All fixtures and temporary files belong to this stage. `validation.json` binds final candidate/source hashes, raw synthetic output and observations, preserved baseline hashes and unchanged production source checks. The first10 observations are retained separately as exploratory evidence; they were not accompanied by a before/after source recorder and are not presented as a source-bound final run.

Run only the synthetic validation from the workspace root with `node work/sketch-collector-next/verify-stage.mjs`. It creates a new owned `synthetic-run-*` directory and retains failures. It never launches the geometry28 suite or writes production results.
